WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.837
Once retrieval is working, we can prepare evidence for the language model.

00:00:04.837 --> 00:00:10.747
A grounded RAG prompt should clearly separate instructions, retrieved context, the user question,

00:00:10.747 --> 00:00:14.607
and the expected answer behavior.

00:00:14.607 --> 00:00:18.472
We tell the model to use only the provided sources.

00:00:18.472 --> 00:00:22.892
We also specify what to do when the evidence is insufficient.

00:00:22.892 --> 00:00:29.342
Instead of inventing an answer, the model should say that the needed information is not available in the retrieved

00:00:29.342 --> 00:00:30.163
context.

00:00:30.803 --> 00:00:34.635
We number the sources so the final response can cite them.

00:00:34.635 --> 00:00:43.085
This does not guarantee perfect factual behavior, but it makes the task much clearer and easier to evaluate.

00:00:43.085 --> 00:00:49.675
A good way to think about RAG prompting is that retrieval decides what evidence is available,

00:00:49.675 --> 00:00:53.110
and the prompt tells the model how to use that evidence.

00:00:53.110 --> 00:00:54.545
Both parts matter.
